WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.481
Classifier-free guidance, or CFG,

00:00:04.561 --> 00:00:08.542
is the workhorse that lets conditional diffusion
models trade sample diversity against fidelity.

00:00:08.622 --> 00:00:12.603
But when the condition is continuous, like a text
embedding,

00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:16.664
CFG ignores that structure.

00:00:16.744 --> 00:00:20.725
This ICLR 2024 paper from Tsinghua University and
Huawei introduces Inner Classifier-Free Guidance,

00:00:20.805 --> 00:00:24.786
or ICFG,

00:00:24.866 --> 00:00:28.847
a perspective that treats standard CFG as the
first-order case of a broader Taylor expansion.

00:00:28.927 --> 00:00:32.908
By adding a second-order term, with no change to training,

00:00:32.988 --> 00:00:36.969
the authors get a better balance between fidelity
and diversity for Stable Diffusion,

00:00:37.049 --> 00:00:41.030
using only a few lines of code.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:48.851
Conditional diffusion models rely on
classifier-free guidance to control how diverse and faithful their samples are.

00:00:48.931 --> 00:00:53.000
The problem is that CFG treats the condition as an
opaque label;

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.112
it places no constraints on the condition space.

00:00:56.192 --> 00:01:00.196
So when that space is continuous, like a text
prompt embedding,

00:01:00.276 --> 00:01:02.401
all that continuity goes to waste.

00:01:02.481 --> 00:01:08.365
The authors ask a pointed question: if the
condition lives in a structured continuous space,

00:01:08.445 --> 00:01:10.440
can we do better than plain CFG?

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:17.203
There are two main ways to inject guidance into
diffusion models: use an external trained classifier,

00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:21.490
or use classifier-free guidance that a single model
learns jointly.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.673
Both work well, but neither says anything about the
shape of the condition space.

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:32.815
The authors' insight is that a text encoder maps
prompts into a continuous space with structure,

00:01:32.895 --> 00:01:36.527
and if you identify that structure, you can move along it.

00:01:36.607 --> 00:01:39.470
That motivates inner classifier-free guidance.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:44.761
The central contribution is a reframing: standard
CFG is not the whole story,

00:01:44.841 --> 00:01:51.761
it is just the first-order term of a more general
expansion the authors call inner classifier-free guidance.

00:01:51.841 --> 00:01:57.141
By writing the guided score as a Taylor series in
the guidance strength around one,

00:01:57.221 --> 00:02:01.937
they recover CFG as the first-order case and then
add a second-order term.

00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:07.446
This term is computed from the existing pretrained
model, so no retraining is needed.

00:02:07.526 --> 00:02:11.400
They also provide a training policy, two sampling
algorithms,

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.150
and a convergence analysis.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.933
Here is the mechanism.

00:02:15.013 --> 00:02:20.662
The authors write the guided distribution as the
unconditional distribution times the

00:02:20.742 --> 00:02:23.223
ratio of conditional to unconditional,

00:02:23.303 --> 00:02:26.796
raised to a power beta, where beta equals w plus one.

00:02:26.876 --> 00:02:30.840
They take a Taylor expansion of the score predictor
in beta,

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.794
evaluated at beta equals one.

00:02:32.874 --> 00:02:36.636
The first-order term is exactly classifier-free guidance.

00:02:36.716 --> 00:02:42.703
The second-order term is new: it is estimated using
a middle point m between zero and one,

00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:47.758
and needs only one extra score evaluation at a
scaled condition, m times c.

00:02:47.838 --> 00:02:51.667
Because everything comes from the already-trained network,

00:02:51.747 --> 00:02:57.329
second-order ICFG drops into a pretrained Stable
Diffusion with a few lines of code,

00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:01.710
controlled by a first-order weight w and a
second-order weight v.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:06.455
The main evaluation is text-to-image on the MS-COCO
validation set.

00:03:06.535 --> 00:03:10.701
Two metrics carry the story: FID, which measures
image fidelity,

00:03:10.781 --> 00:03:13.222
where lower is better, and CLIP Score,

00:03:13.303 --> 00:03:18.331
which measures how well the image matches the
prompt, where higher is better.

00:03:18.411 --> 00:03:21.848
The authors run these on pretrained Stable Diffusion,

00:03:21.928 --> 00:03:29.610
and add a class-conditional experiment on U-ViT
plus a few-shot fine-tuning study on Stable Diffusion one point five.

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:34.291
The headline finding is that the second-order term
genuinely helps.

00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:39.457
On MS-COCO, at guidance strength two with the
second-order weight at a quarter,

00:03:39.537 --> 00:03:45.473
ICFG reaches an FID of fifteen point two eight and
a CLIP score of twenty six point one one.

00:03:45.553 --> 00:03:49.135
That beats classifier-free guidance at the same setting,

00:03:49.215 --> 00:03:55.217
with a worse FID of fifteen point four two and a
lower CLIP score of twenty five point eight.

00:03:55.297 --> 00:03:59.075
So the method improves both fidelity and alignment
at once,

00:03:59.155 --> 00:04:00.775
without touching training.

00:04:00.855 --> 00:04:05.810
The authors identify the sweet spot at w equals two
and v equals one quarter,

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:10.780
and note the full condition space trades off the
two metrics more favorably.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.007
Two ablations illuminate the design choices.

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:18.935
First, the middle point m, used to estimate the
second-order term,

00:04:19.015 --> 00:04:22.863
produces a U-shaped FID curve: if the two points
are too close,

00:04:22.942 --> 00:04:26.790
the estimate can't capture long-term change, and if
they drift too near zero or one,

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:30.718
the model struggles to score them, with the best
value around m equals one point one.

00:04:30.798 --> 00:04:34.645
Second,

00:04:34.725 --> 00:04:38.573
varying the sampling steps shows the method already
produces well-matched images at just ten steps,

00:04:38.653 --> 00:04:42.500
improving modestly up to fifty.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.558
A few numbers to take away.

00:04:46.638 --> 00:04:50.316
The best fidelity-alignment balance on MS-COCO is
an FID of fifteen point two eight and

00:04:50.396 --> 00:04:54.073
a CLIP score of twenty six point one one,

00:04:54.153 --> 00:04:57.831
beating classifier-free guidance on both.

00:04:57.911 --> 00:05:01.589
In the middle-point sweep,

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:05.347
the best FID is fifteen point four two at m equal
to one point one.

00:05:05.427 --> 00:05:09.104
On U-ViT,

00:05:09.184 --> 00:05:12.862
inner classifier-free guidance cuts FID
substantially at low sampling budgets and edges out CFG at high budgets.

00:05:12.942 --> 00:05:16.620
And all of it comes from a few lines of code, with
no change to training.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.399
The takeaway is simple.

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:23.222
Classifier-free guidance is just the first-order
slice of a richer picture.

00:05:23.302 --> 00:05:27.723
Treat the guided score as a Taylor expansion in the
guidance strength,

00:05:27.803 --> 00:05:33.125
and a single extra second-order term buys you a
better fidelity-diversity trade-off.

00:05:33.205 --> 00:05:37.755
It needs no retraining, only a scaled condition and
a few lines of code,

00:05:37.835 --> 00:05:43.800
and it points toward exploiting the continuous
condition structure that plain CFG throws away.
